{
  "revertAcrossUpgrades": {
    "_info": {
      "comment": "Whether a failing call gives back what it did not spend. A helper is called with a fixed 10,000 gas and does nothing but revert. Through Defuse the opcode does not exist, so the call hits an invalid instruction and the whole forwarded amount is consumed -- 10,725 including the call's own cost. From Atlantis the same call consumes 731, because the revert returns the unused gas. THAT DIFFERENCE IS THE RULE: reverting and running out of gas both fail and both undo their state changes, and gas is the only thing that distinguishes them from outside. A fixture checking merely that the call failed would pass identically at every upgrade here. The further step to 2,631 at Magneto is cold account access, not anything about reverting.",
      "oracle": "core-geth production, evm t8n at each upgrade's rules, chain id 61",
      "oracle-version": "v1.12.21-unstable",
      "generated": "2026-08-23",
      "signing": "txbytes carries the signed transaction; the signing key is a filling input, not a fixture field, and the harness reads `sender`.",
      "slots": "0 = the reading. 1 = liveness marker. 2 = gas consumed, where measured.",
      "eip": "EIP-140 at Atlantis",
      "unfilled-no-fork-name": "No expectation for ETC_Thanos: the generator's fork table has no name for them, so their rules cannot be selected. A tooling gap, not a claim that the case does not apply.",
      "unfilled-not-a-state-rule": "No expectation for ETC_DAOFork, ETC_DAOWars, ETC_FrontierThawing, ETC_MESSOff, ETC_MESSOn: these change no state-transition rule, so a state fixture has nothing to assert about them. Covering them needs block-level or chain-selection tests."
    },
